          "lines": 1
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "text": "Lines are formed by grouping glyph runs that share a baseline and a font size. Runs that sit slightly above the baseline, such as footnote markers, are attached to the line they decorate instead of starting a new one. Compound names such as Thoth-Reader keep their hyphen when the line is joined.",
          "rect": [
            72,
            631.3,
            537.9,
            684.3
          ],
          "lines": 4
        },
        {
          "kind": "paragraph",
          "text": "Paragraphs are then assembled from consecutive lines using vertical spacing, indentation and punctuation. A large gap or an indented first line starts a new paragraph, and a short line that ends a sentence usually closes one.",
          "rect": [
            72,
            581.4,
